Title: The Consumption Stimulus Effects of Student Debt Forgiveness Policies
Abstract: The share of liquidity-constrained households holding student debt more than doubled between 2001 and 2016, and student debt relief has become a central policy question. We study student debt forgiveness in a general equilibrium model with the main institutional features of federal student loan repayment. The model matches the marginal propensity to consume out of payment reductions estimated for recent forgiveness programs. We find that forgiveness poses a tradeoff between stimulating the consumption of forgiven borrowers and raising welfare, a tradeoff that does not arise with cash transfers to the same borrowers. Concentrating forgiveness on low-income borrowers, who are the most likely to be constrained, raises welfare but lowers their consumption response. Income-driven repayment (IDR) plans and delinquency explain why. Forgiveness does little to lower the payments of borrowers on IDR plans or in delinquency, who are concentrated at the bottom of the earnings distribution. Removing IDR plans eliminates the tradeoff, while removing delinquency does not.
